\begin{theorem}
Suppose $y\in C^1([a,b])$.  For every interior point $x\in(a,b)$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:first-derivative-notation}
  y'(x)=\frac{dy}{dx}(x).
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both sides of \eqref{eq:first-derivative-notation} denote the unique linear coefficient $L$ for which $y(x+h)=y(x)+Lh+o(h)$ as $h\to0$.  Their equality follows from uniqueness, and the endpoints are excluded only because the displayed expansion is two-sided here.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}
Let $U\subset\mathbb R^n$ be open and $f:U\to\mathbb R$ differentiable.  For every $x\in U$ there is a unique vector $g(x)$ such that
\[
  Df(x)[v]=g(x)\cdot v\qquad\text{for all }v\in\mathbb R^n.
\]
This vector field is the gradient of $f$; hence, after the characterization above, we set $g:=\nabla f$ on $U$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Existence and uniqueness follow from the Euclidean representation of the linear functional $Df(x)$.  The concluding assignment is therefore independent of the choice of direction $v$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Site parameterization}

Coordinate \(x\) increases downgradient. Site measurements provide pore-water velocity \(u\), longitudinal dispersion \(D\), and contaminant half-life \(t_{1/2}\).

\subsection{Governing balance}

The assessment adopts
\[
\frac{\partial C}{\partial t}
=D\frac{\partial^2 C}{\partial x^2}
-u\frac{\partial C}{\partial x}
-\lambda C,
\qquad
\lambda=\frac{\ln 2}{t_{1/2}}.
\]

\subsection{Interpretation}

Although the second derivative also appears in heat and diffusion models, the directed advection and contaminant-decay terms establish the groundwater transport reading used here.

