\subsection{Reviewer proposal}

The reviewer proposed interpreting a fitted coefficient as microbial mortality, citing \(k_m=-(1/N)(dN/dt)\).

\subsection{Authors' fitted model}

Our tracer analysis instead estimates a neutral effective loss coefficient \(k\) in
\[
\frac{dC}{dt}=S(t)-kC.
\]

\subsection{Current disposition}

The present observations do not distinguish microbial mortality from tidal flushing or photolysis, so the revision reports \(k\) without a narrower cause. A planned isotope experiment is the discriminating test.

A charge packet with signed charge $q_b$ entering a region held at potential $V_b$
has electric potential energy
\begin{equation}\tag{1}
U_b=q_bV_b.
\end{equation}
For the dc supply, let $I_s$ be the conventional current delivered at terminal
voltage $V_s$; its output power is
\begin{equation}\tag{2}
P_s=V_sI_s.
\end{equation}
Thus doubling the bias in (1) doubles the packet energy, whereas the same
voltage change in (2) doubles the supply power only if the load current remains
fixed.

\newcommand{\Efield}{\boldsymbol{\mathcal E}}
\newcommand{\freecharge}{\rho_{\!f}}
\newcommand{\permit}{\varepsilon_{\mathrm b}}
In this manuscript, \(\Efield\) is the electric field, \(\freecharge\) is the
free-charge density, and \(\permit\) is the homogeneous background
permittivity.

\input{notation}
In each bulk subdomain, with singular surface charge handled separately by the
interface jump condition, charge conservation is coupled to the electrostatic
solve through Gauss's law,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gauss-local}
  \boldsymbol\nabla\!\cdot\Efield=\frac{\freecharge}{\permit}.
\end{equation}

For a velocity field that is uniform over a cross-section, the volume crossing the
section in a time interval $\Delta t$ is the area $A$ times the travelled distance
$v\Delta t$.  Dividing by that interval gives the volumetric flow rate $Q$,
\begin{equation}
  Q = A v .
  \label{eq:uniform-volume-flux}
\end{equation}
Here $v$ is the section-averaged normal speed; a pointwise centerline speed should
not be substituted into Eq.~\eqref{eq:uniform-volume-flux} unless the profile is
actually uniform.

\newcommand{\mdot}{\dot{m}}
The saline density $\rho$ was measured with an oscillating-tube densitometer at
the test temperature.  For each nozzle, the optical diameter supplied the area
$A$, while particle tracking supplied the area-mean exit speed $v_e$.  We computed
the discharged mass per unit time as
\begin{equation}
  \mdot = \rho A v_e .
  \label{eq:nozzle-mdot}
\end{equation}
This rate, rather than the pump's nominal setting, was used to normalize all
reported solute loads.

In this chapter, $p$ is static pressure, $\rho$ is fluid density, $g$ is
gravitational acceleration, and $z$ is elevation above the laboratory datum.
Using the macros from \texttt{shared/symbols.tex}, total mechanical head is
defined as a decomposition.  Equality of $\Head$ at two stations additionally
requires the usual steady-flow and loss assumptions.  The decomposition is
\begin{equation}
  \Head = \frac{p}{\rho g}+\frac{\vel^2}{2g}+z .
  \label{eq:head-definition}
\end{equation}

Rivera et al. use a dilute-solution closure, but our tank is density-stratified,
its measured flux contains an unresolved advective contribution, and the data do
not separately determine a scalar diffusivity $D$. We therefore report $\mathbf J$
directly and do not adopt the closure. For identification only, the Rivera et al.
model is quoted as
\begin{equation}
  \mathbf{J}=-D\nabla c .
  \label{eq:fick-candidate}
\end{equation}

The ultrasonic meter reports the cross-section mean speed $v$, and the machined
bore determines the wetted area $A$. Liquid density and bore area are effectively
constant over each 30-second acquisition window.  For the liquid phase, the reduction used
throughout the campaign is
\begin{equation}
  Q=A v .
  \label{eq:loop-volume-rate}
\end{equation}
Density was sampled at the same temperature, allowing the corresponding mass rate
to be written as
\begin{equation}
  \dot m=\rho Q=\rho A v .
  \label{eq:loop-mass-rate}
\end{equation}
Both equations refer to time-averaged values over the 30-second acquisition window.

\newcommand{\trainobj}{\mathcal{J}}
Let $L(\theta)$ denote the empirical negative log-likelihood and let
$R(\theta)=\lVert\theta\rVert_2^2/2$ denote the weight penalty.  For a
hyperparameter $\lambda\geq 0$, we minimize the regularized training objective
\begin{equation}\label{eq:penalized-objective}
  \trainobj(\theta)=L(\theta)+\lambda R(\theta).
\end{equation}
When $\lambda=0$, the same definition reduces to unregularized maximum
likelihood training.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:interior-stationarity}
Let $U\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ be open, let $f:U\to\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable,
and suppose that $x^\star\in U$ is a local minimizer of $f$.  Then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:first-order-condition}
  \nabla f(x^\star)=0.
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For every direction $v$, the one-variable function
$t\mapsto f(x^\star+tv)$ has a local minimum at zero, so its derivative there
is $\nabla f(x^\star)^\top v=0$.  Choosing the coordinate directions proves
\eqref{eq:first-order-condition}.
\end{proof}
The conclusion need not hold when $x^\star$ is only a boundary minimizer of a
closed feasible set.

Consider the unconstrained least-squares problem
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ls-problem}
  \min_{\theta\in\mathbb{R}^n}\frac12\lVert A\theta-b\rVert_2^2,
  \qquad A\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n},\quad b\in\mathbb R^m.
\end{equation}
Differentiating the objective gives $A^\top(A\theta-b)$.  Consequently every
least-squares minimizer obeys the normal equation
\begin{equation}\label{eq:normal}
  A^\top A\theta=A^\top b.
\end{equation}
If $A$ has full column rank, this condition identifies the unique minimizer;
otherwise it still characterizes the set of minimizers.

\newcommand{\dtemp}{\Delta T}
For a solid heated at constant pressure with no phase change, take the specific heat
$c_p$ to be constant over the interval.  The temperature rise is
\begin{equation}
  \dtemp = T_2-T_1 .
\end{equation}
The sensible heat absorbed by the specimen is then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sensible-specimen}
  Q = m c_p \dtemp .
\end{equation}
Thus positive $Q$ denotes energy transferred into a specimen for which $T_2>T_1$.

Consider a homogeneous plane wall of area $A$ and thickness $L$ at one-dimensional steady state, with constant conductivity $k$ and no internal heat generation.
Let $\Delta T=T_h-T_c>0$, and define $\dot Q$ as positive from the hot face toward
the cold face.  Integrating the one-dimensional conduction equation gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:wall-rate}
  \dot Q = \frac{kA(T_h-T_c)}{L} = \frac{kA\Delta T}{L}.
\end{equation}
Introducing the wall resistance
\begin{equation}
  R_{\mathrm{th}}=\frac{L}{kA}
\end{equation}
turns Eq.~\eqref{eq:wall-rate} into
\begin{equation}\label{eq:wall-drop}
  \Delta T=\dot Q R_{\mathrm{th}}.
\end{equation}

During each steady plateau, radiative loss was estimated separately and subtracted
from the electrical input; the remainder assigned to the exposed coupon was identified
with its convective heat-transfer rate.  We used the wetted area $A$
and the surface-to-bulk difference $\Delta T=T_s-T_\infty$, retaining only plateaus
with $T_s>T_\infty$.  Newton's cooling relation was written
\begin{equation}\label{eq:newton-cooling}
  \dot Q = hA\Delta T,
\end{equation}
and the reported coefficient followed from
\begin{equation}
  h=\frac{\dot Q}{A\Delta T}.
\end{equation}

The diffusion routine stores heat flux in the global coordinate basis.  For an
isotropic material with scalar conductivity $k>0$, its constitutive update is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fourier-vector}
  \mathbf q=-k\nabla T.
\end{equation}
The minus sign makes the flux point down the temperature gradient.  No surface
area is part of $\mathbf q$; an integrated rate is computed later from
$\int_A \mathbf q\mathbin{\cdot}\mathbf n\,dA$.  If a test field varies only in
$x$, projection of Eq.~\eqref{eq:fourier-vector} gives
\begin{equation}
  q_x=-k\frac{\partial T}{\partial x}.
\end{equation}

Using the conventions in \texttt{shared/signs.md}, the finite-process balance is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:project-first-law}
  U_2-U_1=\qin-\Wby.
\end{equation}
In other words, $\Delta U$ increases with added heat and decreases with work out.

The reduction script first forms the surface-to-stream difference
\begin{equation}
  \Delta T=T_s-T_\infty.
\end{equation}
For each accepted plateau it then records the model product
\begin{equation}\label{eq:pending-product}
  \dot Q=hA\Delta T.
\end{equation}
Here $\dot Q$ is the convective heat rate from the warmer plate to the air, $h$ is
the convection coefficient, and $A$ is the exposed plate area.  This is Newton's
cooling law; plateaus with $T_s\leq T_\infty$ are outside the present heating test.
